138
00:10:47,750 --> 00:10:49,468
So this week,
obviously we've got

139
00:10:49,469 --> 00:10:51,861
the high desert
of California.

140
00:10:51,896 --> 00:10:54,219
- Next week, who knows?
- I do.

141
00:10:54,254 --> 00:10:58,290
Well, yes, James, we know.
It's Johannesburg.

142
00:10:58,325 --> 00:11:01,694
- Now they know.
- I know they know! It doesn't matter!

143
00:11:01,729 --> 00:11:04,765
The point is the view changes
and so does the audience, okay?

144
00:11:04,800 --> 00:11:07,267
This week
you're all Americans.

145
00:11:07,302 --> 00:11:09,302
[CHEERING]

146
00:11:14,742 --> 00:11:17,042
Now, this is a bit
of a problem

147
00:11:17,077 --> 00:11:20,078
for people like us
who speak English.

148
00:11:20,113 --> 00:11:24,351
We can converse perfectly well
in a shop or a restaurant,

149
00:11:24,386 --> 00:11:27,121
but when it comes
to cars and motoring,

150
00:11:27,156 --> 00:11:30,158
every single word you use
is different.

151
00:11:30,193 --> 00:11:33,125
When I say "different",
I mean "wrong."

152
00:11:33,160 --> 00:11:35,562
When you change gear with a
lever, what do you call that?

153
00:11:35,597 --> 00:11:38,096
- Stick.
- Stick.

154
00:11:38,131 --> 00:11:40,266
Now, you see,
this is a stick.

155
00:11:40,301 --> 00:11:42,367
Couldn't change gear
with this.

156
00:11:42,402 --> 00:11:45,105
Wouldn't work. What you can do
with this is point at things.

157
00:11:45,140 --> 00:11:47,140
Let's see
if we can get you educated.

158
00:11:47,159 --> 00:11:49,652
What do you call
this bit of a car?

159
00:11:49,687 --> 00:11:52,184
- ALL: The hood!
- MAN: Bonnet.

160
00:11:52,219 --> 00:11:54,646
- Bonnet.
- They're learning.

161
00:11:54,681 --> 00:11:57,750
What do you mean? I know you
can wear a bonnet on your head.

162
00:11:57,785 --> 00:12:02,455
It's two things. We're capable of
having those different thoughts.

163
00:12:02,490 --> 00:12:04,892
- What type of vehicle
is this? - ALL: Pick-up.

164
00:12:04,927 --> 00:12:06,492
No, it's stupid.

165
00:12:06,493 --> 00:12:09,797
- Oh, now, hang on.
- It is stupid.

166
00:12:09,832 --> 00:12:12,097
Let's do another one of these.

167
00:12:12,132 --> 00:12:16,102
- What type of vehicle is this?
- ALL: Coupe.

168
00:12:16,137 --> 00:12:19,239
Coupe? Coop is where
you keep your chickens.

169
00:12:19,274 --> 00:12:21,892
That's a coupé.
That's a French word, I admit.

170
00:12:21,927 --> 00:12:24,476
- Yeah, a bit.
- They're muddled with that.

171
00:12:24,511 --> 00:12:27,280
George Bush, have you heard what
he once said about the French?

172
00:12:27,315 --> 00:12:29,116
"The trouble with the French is
they have no word

173
00:12:29,117 --> 00:12:31,117
for entrepreneur."

174
00:12:32,252 --> 00:12:34,421
It is difficult. I think
the only thing we agree on

175
00:12:34,456 --> 00:12:36,621
- is "steering wheel."
- Yeah.

176
00:12:36,656 --> 00:12:40,826
But then they go and put it
on the wrong side of the car.

177
00:12:40,861 --> 00:12:45,298
It must be really annoying having to
drive along like that all the time.

178
00:12:45,333 --> 00:12:48,385
Why don't you just put it in
front of the driver like we do?

179
00:12:48,420 --> 00:12:51,438
It's probably a good idea if
we get on with the actual show.

180
00:12:51,473 --> 00:12:53,806
Yes, it is. But we had
to clear all that up,

181
00:12:53,841 --> 00:12:56,176
because contrary to what
some people have been saying,

182
00:12:56,211 --> 00:12:58,543
this actually is a car show.

183
00:12:58,578 --> 00:13:01,314
- Well, not next week it isn't.
- Next week is an exception.

184
00:13:01,349 --> 00:13:03,284
- Yeah.
- But not when we go to Barbados either.

185
00:13:03,285 --> 00:13:06,137
No, Barbados is definitely
another exception.

186
00:13:06,172 --> 00:13:08,954
But tonight couldn't be
more "car-y" if it tried.

187
00:13:08,989 --> 00:13:12,159
I'm gonna prove that point because
we're kicking off with this.

188
00:13:13,861 --> 00:13:16,161
[LAUGHTER AND JEERING]

189
00:13:16,196 --> 00:13:19,065
Bear with me, bear with me-

190
00:13:19,100 --> 00:13:21,101
- I agree!
- [LAUGHTER]

191
00:13:21,136 --> 00:13:23,102
The thing is, though,

192
00:13:23,103 --> 00:13:25,470
various supercar makers
have now taken

193
00:13:25,505 --> 00:13:29,307
the polar-bear-friendly
technology from a Prius,

194
00:13:29,342 --> 00:13:33,146
and they are now using it
to create raw, naked speed.

195
00:13:33,181 --> 00:13:36,416
Now, I say the best
of these hybrid hypercars

196
00:13:36,451 --> 00:13:39,670
is the mad,
swivel-eyed McLaren P1.

197
00:13:39,705 --> 00:13:42,890
Whereas Richard Hammond,
who's wrong-

198
00:13:42,925 --> 00:13:43,990
I'm not wrong.

199
00:13:43,991 --> 00:13:46,224
He is, because he says
the best

200
00:13:46,259 --> 00:13:49,462
is actually the rather
boring Porsche 918.

201
00:13:49,497 --> 00:13:51,999
- It's not boring!
- Yes, it is.

202
00:13:52,034 --> 00:13:53,818
- It isn't.
- Mm-hmm.

203
00:13:53,819 --> 00:13:55,534
It isn't.
Well, whatever.

204
00:13:55,535 --> 00:13:58,554
We decided to meet up
with the cars

205
00:13:58,589 --> 00:14:01,574
and settle this
once and for all.

206
00:14:59,332 --> 00:15:04,254
The location we chose for this
titanic duel was Portugal...

207
00:15:04,289 --> 00:15:09,176
specifically, the international
race track of the Algarve.

208
00:15:15,515 --> 00:15:18,318
When we arrived,
mechanics from Porsche

209
00:15:18,353 --> 00:15:20,938
and McLaren pounced on
our cars,

210
00:15:20,973 --> 00:15:23,488
fettling them for
what was to come.

211
00:15:23,523 --> 00:15:26,593
And this meant Hammond and I
had some time to indulge

212
00:15:26,628 --> 00:15:30,562
in a spot
of childish point scoring.

213
00:15:30,597 --> 00:15:33,833
Which one of these is going to
be the fastest around this track?

214
00:15:33,868 --> 00:15:35,234
- This?
- Yeah.

215
00:15:35,235 --> 00:15:37,502
Apart from this one,
which will be faster.

216
00:15:37,537 --> 00:15:40,173
It won't. Which one do you
think will be the fastest?

217
00:15:40,208 --> 00:15:42,108
- This one.
- This one.

218
00:15:42,109 --> 00:15:45,412
Porsche think the Porsche will be
fastest. McLaren say the McLaren.

219
00:15:45,447 --> 00:15:47,512
How much is it
if I wanted to buy one?

220
00:15:47,547 --> 00:15:51,084
I think one was sold in
an auction for 1.8 million.

221
00:15:51,119 --> 00:15:54,621
1.8 million dollars.
Hammond, how much is yours?

222
00:15:54,656 --> 00:15:56,622
838 euros.

223
00:15:56,623 --> 00:15:59,492
- Thousand I'm guessing?
- Yes. It is thousands.

224
00:15:59,527 --> 00:16:02,362
Why has your car
got a number on the side?

225
00:16:02,397 --> 00:16:04,362
It's the Weissach edition,
this one.

226
00:16:04,397 --> 00:16:06,433
- You also get titanium chassis bolts.
- Stupid.

227
00:16:06,468 --> 00:16:08,533
For just an extra ã60.000.

228
00:16:08,568 --> 00:16:12,370
And a carbon-fiber
bonnet strut.

229
00:16:12,405 --> 00:16:13,805
Look at that.
How much does that save?

230
00:16:13,806 --> 00:16:16,792
- About 100 grams.
- Right there, 100 grams.

231
00:16:16,827 --> 00:16:19,744
Has the McLaren got
a carbon-fiber bonnet strut?

232
00:16:19,779 --> 00:16:23,150
- Doesn't need one.
- No, because my bonnet doesn't go up

233
00:16:23,185 --> 00:16:25,517
because it will never
break down.

234
00:16:25,552 --> 00:16:28,488
And it's got silly doors.
Proper doors, silly doors.

235
00:16:28,523 --> 00:16:32,125
Mine does 34 miles
to the gallon.

236
00:16:32,160 --> 00:16:34,359
This does 94.

237
00:16:34,394 --> 00:16:36,562
- 94 miles to the gallon?
- 94mpg.

238
00:16:36,597 --> 00:16:38,731
Just remind me,
Porsche is owned by?

239
00:16:38,766 --> 00:16:40,766
That'll be Volkswagen.

240
00:16:46,106 --> 00:16:50,877
Soon the McLaren and the Porsche
were ready for some shakedown laps.

241
00:16:50,912 --> 00:16:53,545
[REVVING]

242
00:16:53,580 --> 00:16:58,151
And for these we decided
we'd drive each other's cars.

243
00:17:02,489 --> 00:17:04,290
Straight away,
I can tell you

244
00:17:04,291 --> 00:17:07,760
that this doesn't have
the brutality or the savagery

245
00:17:07,795 --> 00:17:10,662
of the McLaren.

246
00:17:10,697 --> 00:17:14,734
Because it has rear-wheel
steering and four-wheel drive,

247
00:17:14,769 --> 00:17:17,602
it feels secure and safe.

248
00:17:17,637 --> 00:17:19,839
And that gives you
the confidence-

249
00:17:19,874 --> 00:17:22,274
[CHUCKLES]

250
00:17:22,309 --> 00:17:24,411
...to play about.

251
00:17:28,715 --> 00:17:30,715
[TIRES SQUEALING]

252
00:17:33,920 --> 00:17:38,591
If I went round this corner
this fast in the McLaren...

253
00:17:40,927 --> 00:17:43,463
...a lot of poo would come out.

254
00:17:49,369 --> 00:17:52,239
Whoa! There's poo
coming out now!

255
00:17:55,508 --> 00:17:57,508
Jesus.

256
00:17:58,545 --> 00:18:01,414
It's spinning its
[BLEEP] wheels in sixth!

257
00:18:06,453 --> 00:18:08,821
This is frightening,
that's what it is.

258
00:18:11,324 --> 00:18:14,126
Whoa! More poo.

259
00:18:14,161 --> 00:18:18,331
I didn't think it was possible
to shit yourself to death.

260
00:18:23,903 --> 00:18:25,903
[CHUCKLES]

261
00:18:31,711 --> 00:18:35,182
The grip is genuinely
incredible.

262
00:18:39,419 --> 00:18:41,986
Oh, there you go.

263
00:18:42,021 --> 00:18:45,192
RICHARD: In the savage
and unforgiving P1,

264
00:18:45,227 --> 00:18:47,995
my nerves were shot
to pieces.

265
00:18:48,030 --> 00:18:50,763
But I, too, was falling
in love.

266
00:18:50,798 --> 00:18:53,366
903 horsepower.

267
00:18:55,968 --> 00:18:57,968
Oh, my God!

268
00:19:00,072 --> 00:19:02,209
Oh, you bad, bad,
naughty car!

269
00:19:06,879 --> 00:19:08,879
Jesus.

270
00:19:10,550 --> 00:19:12,817
This recalibrates
your mind.

271
00:19:12,852 --> 00:19:16,088
I didn't think anything could
be as exciting as that Porsche,

272
00:19:16,123 --> 00:19:18,708
but this- this is.

273
00:19:18,743 --> 00:19:21,294
[TIRES SCREECH]

274
00:19:22,395 --> 00:19:24,397
This is brilliant.

275
00:19:25,965 --> 00:19:30,002
Absolutely brilliant.

276
00:19:30,037 --> 00:19:32,037
Oh.

277
00:19:32,038 --> 00:19:33,840
What a car.

278
00:19:33,841 --> 00:19:35,841
I love it.

279
00:19:38,245 --> 00:19:41,045
- Well?
- This is rubbish!

280
00:19:41,080 --> 00:19:43,450
I was just thinking exactly
the same about this.

281
00:19:43,485 --> 00:19:45,150
- Were you?
- This was boring me to death.

282
00:19:45,151 --> 00:19:49,621
It was like being stuck
in a Victorian woman's novel.

283
00:19:49,656 --> 00:19:53,526
I'd rather that than being stuck in a
telephone box with a panicking gorilla.

284
00:19:53,561 --> 00:19:55,561
- Rubbish.
- It was trying to kill me.

285
00:19:55,562 --> 00:19:57,597
- It wants to hurt me.
- That's why I like it.

286
00:19:57,632 --> 00:19:59,800
This is
a missionary-position car.

287
00:19:59,835 --> 00:20:02,536
Razor blade
in the hands of a surgeon.

288
00:20:02,571 --> 00:20:05,203
Sickle in the hands
of a drunk peasant.

289
00:20:05,238 --> 00:20:08,908
Downton Abbey. Breaking Bad.
That's crystal meth.

290
00:20:08,943 --> 00:20:10,576
That's some iffy weed.

291
00:20:10,577 --> 00:20:14,213
Fine wine.
Crate of Newcy Brown.

292
00:20:15,282 --> 00:20:18,318
Schooner of sherry.
Absinthe.

293
00:20:18,353 --> 00:20:20,085
James Bond specifying

294
00:20:20,086 --> 00:20:22,121
his martini to be shaken
not stirred.

295
00:20:22,156 --> 00:20:23,655
Begbie in Trainspotting

296
00:20:23,656 --> 00:20:25,492
lobbing a pint pot over
his shoulder in a night club

297
00:20:25,493 --> 00:20:28,261
and starting a bar fight.
What?

298
00:20:33,165 --> 00:20:34,833
Hi.

299
00:20:34,834 --> 00:20:36,803
What are you doing here?

300
00:20:36,804 --> 00:20:38,704
I'm here because, gentlemen,

301
00:20:38,705 --> 00:20:40,705
I have in the back
of that lorry

302
00:20:40,740 --> 00:20:43,576
something that will make
your McLaren and your Porsche

303
00:20:43,611 --> 00:20:45,978
look like lumps of yesteryear.

304
00:20:52,084 --> 00:20:54,084
[REVVING]

305
00:21:13,973 --> 00:21:15,909
I cannot remember
the last time a car,

306
00:21:15,910 --> 00:21:17,776
or, indeed,
any sort of thing,

307
00:21:17,777 --> 00:21:20,580
gave me a fizz like
the Ferrari LaFerrari.

308
00:21:22,349 --> 00:21:25,385
This has 950 horsepower.

309
00:21:25,420 --> 00:21:27,420
950.

310
00:21:28,355 --> 00:21:30,188
More than either
of the other two.

311
00:21:30,189 --> 00:21:32,992
And it's lighter.
It weighs 1.250 kilograms.

312
00:21:35,928 --> 00:21:38,898
Which is less
than most hatchbacks.

313
00:21:41,601 --> 00:21:43,835
Actually there is a bit more
to the Ferrari LaFerrari,

314
00:21:43,870 --> 00:21:48,708
because what it has is, in
effect, a formula one KERS system.

315
00:21:48,743 --> 00:21:53,546
It constantly recovers energy
that would otherwise be lost

316
00:21:53,581 --> 00:21:55,948
using its electric motor
and its battery.

317
00:21:55,983 --> 00:21:58,716
So as I brake there,

318
00:21:58,751 --> 00:22:02,622
the car is actually saying,
"That energy, I'll have that."

319
00:22:03,890 --> 00:22:05,890
Straight!

320
00:22:07,026 --> 00:22:10,630
This is what 950 horsepower
feels like!

321
00:22:15,301 --> 00:22:17,437
Jesus!

322
00:22:21,273 --> 00:22:25,678
This I believe will absolutely
mince the other two.

323
00:22:25,713 --> 00:22:27,713
Oh, man!

324
00:22:29,315 --> 00:22:32,485
JEREMY: James was obviously
talking nonsense.

325
00:22:32,520 --> 00:22:34,752
But there's no getting
around the fact

326
00:22:34,787 --> 00:22:37,023
that these three cars
take automotive science

327
00:22:37,058 --> 00:22:39,058
to a new level.

328
00:22:41,260 --> 00:22:43,996
They use
the latest green technology

329
00:22:44,031 --> 00:22:46,733
to squeeze
as much speed as possible

330
00:22:46,768 --> 00:22:49,536
from every molecule of fuel.

331
00:22:51,070 --> 00:22:54,240
As a result, they're
all capable of blasting

332
00:22:54,275 --> 00:22:56,641
way past 200 miles an hour

333
00:22:56,676 --> 00:23:00,947
whilst producing fewer harmful
emissions than a family saloon.

334
00:23:06,352 --> 00:23:11,057
What we have here, then,
are three incredible machines

335
00:23:11,092 --> 00:23:12,891
which, at a stroke,

336
00:23:12,892 --> 00:23:16,128
have made the traditional
supercar look wooden...

337
00:23:16,163 --> 00:23:18,163
and old-fashioned.

338
00:23:20,867 --> 00:23:24,637
Welcome, everyone,
to the hypercar holy trinity.

339
00:23:28,040 --> 00:23:32,461
In that door mirror
I have a ã1 million Porsche.

340
00:23:32,496 --> 00:23:36,883
In that door mirror
I have a ã1 million Ferrari.

341
00:23:39,218 --> 00:23:41,218
What a toy box today.

342
00:23:47,660 --> 00:23:49,796
Sparks from Jezzer.

343
00:23:56,268 --> 00:23:58,238
I don't think so, Hammond.

344
00:23:58,239 --> 00:24:00,239
[TIRES SQUEALING]

345
00:24:05,745 --> 00:24:07,880
This could be so expensive.

346
00:24:17,724 --> 00:24:19,926
It's going to boost.

347
00:24:19,961 --> 00:24:22,093
Pushing the IPAS button!

348
00:24:22,128 --> 00:24:26,132
And that gives me
another 170 horsepower.

349
00:24:26,167 --> 00:24:28,167
[LAUGHING]

350
00:24:28,935 --> 00:24:30,935
Opening DRS!

351
00:24:32,539 --> 00:24:34,541
Oh, my Christ!

352
00:24:34,576 --> 00:24:36,874
Bloody...

353
00:24:36,909 --> 00:24:39,746
And here comes May
in the Ferrari LaFerrari.

354
00:24:44,984 --> 00:24:47,954
Concentrating.
Concentrating.

355
00:24:59,832 --> 00:25:02,199
Oh-oh! That bloody car

356
00:25:02,234 --> 00:25:04,236
is trying to kill me,
and I'm not even in it.

357
00:25:07,139 --> 00:25:09,139
Ha-ha!

358
00:25:19,586 --> 00:25:22,839
JEREMY: We could have played
out there all day,

359
00:25:22,874 --> 00:25:26,092
but before we ended up
in a ã3 million crash,

360
00:25:26,127 --> 00:25:28,092
we decided to start
the tests

361
00:25:28,127 --> 00:25:30,963
to see which
of the cars was best.

362
00:25:30,998 --> 00:25:32,631
I came up with the first one,

363
00:25:32,632 --> 00:25:35,868
mainly to annoy
gate-crasher May.

364
00:25:35,903 --> 00:25:39,070
Let's make the first test
a drag race

365
00:25:39,105 --> 00:25:43,042
- using electrical power only.
- Good idea.

366
00:25:43,077 --> 00:25:45,743
- It isn't a good idea.
- Why not?

367
00:25:45,778 --> 00:25:48,615
Because you can't drive the
Ferrari on electrical power only.

368
00:25:48,650 --> 00:25:51,583
- No!
- Of course you can't,

369
00:25:51,618 --> 00:25:53,686
because it's a KERS system
like a Formula One car.

370
00:25:53,721 --> 00:25:56,588
It's got a V12 engine
and an electric motor,

371
00:25:56,623 --> 00:25:59,876
but they are integrated, they work
together, you can't separate them.

372
00:25:59,911 --> 00:26:03,129
- You should have thought about that, shouldn't you?
- Bad planning.

373
00:26:04,831 --> 00:26:07,599
With James reduced
to the role of onlooker,

374
00:26:07,634 --> 00:26:11,454
we lined up the P1 and
the 918 on the start line,

375
00:26:11,489 --> 00:26:15,274
but then, instead of
revving the petrol engines,

376
00:26:15,309 --> 00:26:17,644
we shut them down.

377
00:26:21,848 --> 00:26:23,848
That is weird.

378
00:26:23,883 --> 00:26:27,186
Preparing to start a drag race

379
00:26:27,221 --> 00:26:29,520
in complete silence.

380
00:26:29,555 --> 00:26:32,156
I still have
two electric motors

381
00:26:32,191 --> 00:26:35,527
that together produce
285 brake horsepower

382
00:26:35,562 --> 00:26:37,162
which is 109 more

383
00:26:37,163 --> 00:26:42,301
than the one electric motor
in his McLaren. Oh, yeah.

384
00:26:43,535 --> 00:26:46,172
[BEEPING]

385
00:26:47,273 --> 00:26:49,273
[BEEP AND KLAXON]

386
00:26:52,679 --> 00:26:54,812
I'm outta here!

387
00:26:54,847 --> 00:26:57,483
You've got to love that,
the immediate power

388
00:26:57,518 --> 00:26:59,884
from an electrical engine.

389
00:26:59,919 --> 00:27:03,956
That is 70 miles an hour
already and it isn't enough.

390
00:27:03,991 --> 00:27:05,991
[RICHARD LAUGHING]

391
00:27:08,327 --> 00:27:10,697
[LAUGHING]

392
00:27:16,703 --> 00:27:19,737
Who won that?
It was me, wasn't it?

393
00:27:19,772 --> 00:27:21,808
Yeah, but it's not
important though, is it?

394
00:27:21,843 --> 00:27:23,609
- Is it not?
- It's just not relevant.

395
00:27:23,610 --> 00:27:25,176
Right, right.

396
00:27:25,177 --> 00:27:27,880
In a drag race, it's irrelevant
which car gets there first?

397
00:27:27,915 --> 00:27:31,117
Yeah, yours is
the better milk float.

398
00:27:31,152 --> 00:27:33,550
[LAUGHS]

399
00:27:33,585 --> 00:27:37,989
Back in the pits, Hammond had
an idea for the next test.

400
00:27:38,024 --> 00:27:40,877
We've got to drive to the hotel
yet. It's about an hour away.

401
00:27:40,912 --> 00:27:43,730
It gives us a chance to see
what they're like on the road.

402
00:27:43,765 --> 00:27:46,130
- That's a good point.
- Real world.

403
00:27:46,165 --> 00:27:48,717
- That is a good point.
- I can't do that.

404
00:27:48,752 --> 00:27:51,270
I can't drive the Ferrari
on the road.

405
00:27:51,305 --> 00:27:53,323
- Why?
- It's not registered.

406
00:27:53,358 --> 00:27:55,340
It is,
it's got number plates.

407
00:27:55,341 --> 00:27:57,208
No, that's just pretend
number plates.

408
00:27:57,209 --> 00:27:59,144
If they register it,
it becomes second-hand

409
00:27:59,145 --> 00:28:00,981
and they pay tax. That's
why it came in the lorry.

410
00:28:00,982 --> 00:28:02,999
So you can't drive it
on the road either?

411
00:28:03,034 --> 00:28:05,034
Nobody can drive it.
It's not road legal.

412
00:28:05,052 --> 00:28:07,719
- Oh, no.
- Mate, that's...

413
00:28:07,754 --> 00:28:10,356
- That is such a shame.
- It's an hour of-

414
00:28:10,391 --> 00:28:12,456
That's really a rotten bit
of luck.

415
00:28:12,491 --> 00:28:14,626
'Cause you've come a long way
from Italy.

416
00:28:14,661 --> 00:28:16,494
I can't give you a lift

417
00:28:16,495 --> 00:28:19,365
'cause the soundman's headphones
are on the seat and the mixer.

418
00:28:19,400 --> 00:28:21,166
JAMES: Oh, no.

419
00:28:21,167 --> 00:28:23,167
There's literally
nothing I can do.

420
00:28:26,338 --> 00:28:29,058
Soon Richard
and I were enjoying

421
00:28:29,093 --> 00:28:31,778
the world's
best-ever commute.

422
00:28:39,218 --> 00:28:41,504
It may be a bit Spartan
in here,

423
00:28:41,539 --> 00:28:43,790
but it's unbelievably
comfortable.

424
00:28:44,690 --> 00:28:47,925
Because it uses electronics

425
00:28:47,960 --> 00:28:50,212
rather than traditional
anti-roll bars.

426
00:28:50,247 --> 00:28:52,464
That means there's
no physical connection

427
00:28:52,499 --> 00:28:54,299
between the wheels.

428
00:28:54,300 --> 00:28:57,886
So if one goes over a bump
the others aren't affected.

429
00:28:57,921 --> 00:29:01,473
It's uncanny. It's like being
in a Rolls-Royce Phantom.

430
00:29:04,977 --> 00:29:08,714
It's a lovely evening.
Taken the roof panels out.

431
00:29:08,749 --> 00:29:10,782
Set the air-con just so.

432
00:29:10,817 --> 00:29:12,919
And I'm enjoying
a drive in the country.

433
00:29:21,393 --> 00:29:23,393
[CHUCKLES]

434
00:29:26,532 --> 00:29:28,532
JEREMY: Meanwhile...

435
00:29:57,997 --> 00:29:59,997
[CHUCKLES]

436
00:30:03,069 --> 00:30:06,272
JAMES: Oh, bloody hell.

437
00:30:06,307 --> 00:30:08,307
[BEEPING]

438
00:30:09,375 --> 00:30:11,209
You're all right.

439
00:30:11,210 --> 00:30:13,679
You're all right,
in Italian.

440
00:30:13,714 --> 00:30:15,814
[CAR HORN]

441
00:30:18,750 --> 00:30:20,887
[CAR HORNS]

442
00:30:22,354 --> 00:30:24,354
Oh, God.

443
00:30:24,374 --> 00:30:26,374
[CHEERING]

444
00:30:30,362 --> 00:30:32,330
- James.
- What?

445
00:30:32,331 --> 00:30:35,902
I'm sorry to have to say this,
but that Ferrari is useless.

446
00:30:35,937 --> 00:30:38,438
Yes, yes, yes.
Later on in part two,

447
00:30:38,473 --> 00:30:40,940
when these two have stopped
dreaming up

448
00:30:40,975 --> 00:30:42,640
idiotic and irrelevant tests

449
00:30:42,641 --> 00:30:46,478
that it can't take part in,
we shall see.

450
00:30:46,513 --> 00:30:48,313
Fair enough. Now,

451
00:30:48,314 --> 00:30:50,781
at this point in the show
we like to sit down

452
00:30:50,816 --> 00:30:54,251
and chew the fat about new
cars and motoring issues.

453
00:30:54,286 --> 00:30:57,955
Like, for example, why in Britain
we have to drive at 50 miles an hour

454
00:30:57,990 --> 00:31:01,627
when they're doing roadworks to protect
the workforce which is never there.

455
00:31:01,662 --> 00:31:03,328
Yes, okay...

456
00:31:03,329 --> 00:31:05,329
And why fully-grown adults

457
00:31:05,331 --> 00:31:08,767
have taken to riding around
the city on children's toys?

458
00:31:08,802 --> 00:31:11,320
- Yes, okay.
- I think they're called bicycles.

459
00:31:11,355 --> 00:31:14,282
- Is that the right word?
- It's issues like that.

460
00:31:14,317 --> 00:31:17,209
It's a segment we are calling
Conversation Street.

461
00:31:17,244 --> 00:31:19,345
â«â« [JAZZY TUNE]

462
00:31:22,848 --> 00:31:24,615
[LAUGHTER]

463
00:31:24,616 --> 00:31:27,653
- Well...
- What?

464
00:31:27,688 --> 00:31:29,653
[CHEERING]

465
00:31:29,688 --> 00:31:31,989
We put a lot of work into that!

466
00:31:32,024 --> 00:31:35,927
It was really,
really hard doing that.

467
00:31:35,962 --> 00:31:39,365
Better than that, rather
brilliantly, we have made

468
00:31:39,400 --> 00:31:41,733
a bespoke version
of that sting

469
00:31:41,768 --> 00:31:43,268
for every single episode

470
00:31:43,269 --> 00:31:45,504
we're gonna make
for the next three years.

471
00:31:45,539 --> 00:31:47,539
How about that?

472
00:31:47,574 --> 00:31:49,606
[CHEERING]

473
00:31:49,641 --> 00:31:51,877
But hold on.
The slight problem with that

474
00:31:51,912 --> 00:31:53,711
is that we were
so busy doing that

475
00:31:53,712 --> 00:31:56,682
we didn't think actually
of anything to talk about.

476
00:31:56,717 --> 00:31:58,516
- We did.
- Yeah.

477
00:31:58,517 --> 00:32:00,717
- What?
- You'll love this.

478
00:32:00,752 --> 00:32:06,658
James May, the world's
slowest moving human being,

479
00:32:06,693 --> 00:32:10,029
has been caught speeding.

480
00:32:10,064 --> 00:32:12,663
[GASPS OF SURPRISE]

481
00:32:12,698 --> 00:32:14,534
Actually charged
by the police.

482
00:32:14,535 --> 00:32:17,134
Criminal.

483
00:32:17,169 --> 00:32:18,703
He's actually quite proud of it.

484
00:32:18,704 --> 00:32:22,208
He did it on a Honda
CBR 600RR sports bike.

485
00:32:23,909 --> 00:32:25,176
The thing was...

486
00:32:25,177 --> 00:32:27,578
[OVERLAPPING CHATTER]

487
00:32:27,613 --> 00:32:30,316
Richard and I were
genuinely astonished.

488
00:32:30,351 --> 00:32:32,286
He came into the office
and said,

489
00:32:32,287 --> 00:32:34,152
"I've been done
for speeding."

490
00:32:34,153 --> 00:32:37,123
Until we found out
how fast he was going.

491
00:32:37,158 --> 00:32:39,558
James, how fast was it again?

492
00:32:39,593 --> 00:32:42,495
37 miles an hour.

493
00:32:42,530 --> 00:32:45,362
Crazy! Mad man!

494
00:32:45,397 --> 00:32:48,450
How did you even breathe
at that kind of speed?

495
00:32:48,485 --> 00:32:51,503
How did the police catch you?
Were they running?

496
00:32:52,938 --> 00:32:55,339
Gentle jog for sure.

497
00:32:55,374 --> 00:32:57,374
The thing is,
if you've been caught,

498
00:32:57,409 --> 00:33:00,996
'cause we genuinely don't
believe anyone in the world has,

499
00:33:01,031 --> 00:33:04,583
but if you've been caught
speeding at a speed less than 37,

500
00:33:04,618 --> 00:33:07,653
do honestly please write
to us and let us know.

501
00:33:07,688 --> 00:33:09,653
'Cause we don't think
it's possible.

502
00:33:09,688 --> 00:33:12,174
And mark your envelope,
"I'm a tortoise."

503
00:33:12,209 --> 00:33:14,660
And that is all
we have time for this week

504
00:33:14,695 --> 00:33:16,094
on Conversation Street.

505
00:33:16,095 --> 00:33:17,462
- Whoa! Hang on a minute.
- What?

506
00:33:17,463 --> 00:33:19,415
The first-ever show,
and you just mock me

507
00:33:19,416 --> 00:33:21,334
for my speeding ticket
and then we move on?

508
00:33:21,335 --> 00:33:23,301
It's a busy show.

509
00:33:23,302 --> 00:33:26,372
It is a busy show. We've got to
tell everyone about our track.

510
00:33:26,407 --> 00:33:29,406
- Yes, we have one.
- [CHEERING]

511
00:33:29,441 --> 00:33:32,378
This is the sort of place
where you can drive cars

512
00:33:32,413 --> 00:33:34,345
at speeds that you can do
on the road

513
00:33:34,346 --> 00:33:36,948
but only if you want
to go to prison afterwards.

514
00:33:36,983 --> 00:33:38,749
What, like 38 miles an hour?

515
00:33:38,750 --> 00:33:42,419
39 maybe even,
40 from time to time.

516
00:33:42,454 --> 00:33:44,454
Now, we were hoping
that we could bring it

517
00:33:44,474 --> 00:33:46,459
round the world with us
like the tent.

518
00:33:46,460 --> 00:33:50,194
But unfortunately
it's too heavy,

519
00:33:50,229 --> 00:33:53,099
and, as you are about to see,
too bitey.

520
00:33:57,869 --> 00:33:59,870
This is it.

521
00:33:59,905 --> 00:34:02,374
It's not a race circuit.

522
00:34:02,409 --> 00:34:04,843
It's not an airfield.

523
00:34:04,878 --> 00:34:07,344
It's not a road.

524
00:34:07,379 --> 00:34:10,716
What it is
is brilliant and fast.

525
00:34:11,950 --> 00:34:14,120
And extremely dangerous.

526
00:34:15,554 --> 00:34:17,788
It even looks dangerous
on a map.

527
00:34:17,823 --> 00:34:21,160
Because as you can see,
it's the exact same shape

528
00:34:21,195 --> 00:34:23,295
as the Ebola virus.

529
00:34:25,564 --> 00:34:29,351
Right, time now to show you
what a lap looks like.

530
00:34:29,386 --> 00:34:33,139
To do that we've got a bit of
a performance benchmark.

531
00:34:33,174 --> 00:34:35,174
A Ferrari 488.

532
00:34:35,193 --> 00:34:37,193
Take it away.

533
00:34:37,220 --> 00:34:39,033
[REVVING]

534
00:34:39,034 --> 00:34:41,034
Take it away.

535
00:34:43,582 --> 00:34:46,518
First up,
it's the Isn't Straight,

536
00:34:46,553 --> 00:34:49,420
so called because
it isn't straight.

537
00:34:49,455 --> 00:34:53,659
There is a super-fast left
followed by a super-fast right.

538
00:34:53,694 --> 00:34:57,863
And if you make a mistake,
there's no run-off area or Armco.

539
00:34:57,898 --> 00:35:01,132
It's just woods.

540
00:35:01,167 --> 00:35:03,167
Woods that are full of wildlife

541
00:35:03,169 --> 00:35:05,871
which likes to run out
in front of you.

542
00:35:08,240 --> 00:35:11,410
After the second right
on the Isn't Straight,

543
00:35:11,445 --> 00:35:14,180
we're into
Your Name Here Corner.

544
00:35:15,714 --> 00:35:18,450
Sponsorship opportunities
are available here.

545
00:35:18,485 --> 00:35:20,485
However, I should explain

546
00:35:20,486 --> 00:35:23,589
that it's possible nobody
will be looking at your banner

547
00:35:23,624 --> 00:35:25,957
because this is a corner
that was designed

548
00:35:25,992 --> 00:35:28,427
so a car can do this.

549
00:35:40,339 --> 00:35:41,839
[TIRES SCREECH]

550
00:35:41,840 --> 00:35:43,107
Ladies and gentlemen,

551
00:35:43,108 --> 00:35:45,393
James May...
isn't here today.

552
00:35:45,428 --> 00:35:47,644
That's just a friend
of mine

553
00:35:47,679 --> 00:35:51,850
on his way back up
the Isn't Straight...

554
00:35:51,885 --> 00:35:54,220
towards Old Lady's House.

555
00:35:55,787 --> 00:35:58,457
We've called this section
Old Lady's House,

556
00:35:58,492 --> 00:36:00,492
because it's right next
to a house

557
00:36:00,527 --> 00:36:03,762
where an old lady lives.

558
00:36:03,797 --> 00:36:06,816
She tells us she likes cars.

559
00:36:06,851 --> 00:36:09,800
Says she used to have one.

560
00:36:09,835 --> 00:36:12,771
Not sure she's going
to like them quite so much

561
00:36:12,806 --> 00:36:14,806
after we've been here
a few times.

562
00:36:14,808 --> 00:36:17,874
[TIRES SQUEALING]

563
00:36:17,909 --> 00:36:20,779
Next up, there's a narrow
and bumpy sprint

564
00:36:20,814 --> 00:36:23,480
to Sub Station.

565
00:36:23,515 --> 00:36:25,849
You need to get
this 90-left right,

566
00:36:25,884 --> 00:36:28,887
or you'll crash into
a cage full of electricity.

567
00:36:31,056 --> 00:36:34,360
And you'll need to get the
final 90-left right as well,

568
00:36:34,395 --> 00:36:36,928
or you'll run into
a field of sheep.

569
00:36:40,799 --> 00:36:42,867
So, trees, animals,

570
00:36:42,902 --> 00:36:44,902
a house, electricity,

571
00:36:44,936 --> 00:36:46,771
and because it's in England,

572
00:36:46,772 --> 00:36:48,905
usually quite a lot
of moisture in the air.

573
00:36:48,940 --> 00:36:53,345
It really is the most dangerous
track anywhere in the world.

574
00:36:53,380 --> 00:36:55,381
To post a fast time here,

575
00:36:55,416 --> 00:36:59,017
you do need...

576
00:36:59,052 --> 00:37:01,052
those.

577
00:37:06,758 --> 00:37:09,728
The car I've selected
for my first lap

578
00:37:09,763 --> 00:37:12,364
of the Eboladrome
is this.

579
00:37:15,967 --> 00:37:18,404
The BMW M2.

580
00:37:32,784 --> 00:37:35,987
Sadly, there isn't the time
in this, our first-ever show,

581
00:37:36,022 --> 00:37:39,458
to do a full road test
of this car,

582
00:37:39,493 --> 00:37:43,145
but there is time
to say that I think

583
00:37:43,180 --> 00:37:46,798
this is the best M car
BMW has ever made.

584
00:38:00,346 --> 00:38:02,346
In a lot of fast cars
these days,

585
00:38:02,381 --> 00:38:04,550
you get all sorts
of knobs and buttons

586
00:38:04,585 --> 00:38:06,619
which you can use
to change-

587
00:38:06,654 --> 00:38:08,619
I say change-
I mean ruin

588
00:38:08,654 --> 00:38:10,087
the characteristics
of the engine

589
00:38:10,088 --> 00:38:12,624
and the suspension
and the steering and so on.

590
00:38:12,659 --> 00:38:15,160
And I always think,
isn't that like a chef

591
00:38:15,195 --> 00:38:16,994
bringing a fish to your table

592
00:38:16,995 --> 00:38:19,598
and an oven and some butter
and a bit of parsley,

593
00:38:19,633 --> 00:38:21,801
and saying,
"Cook it yourself"? No.

594
00:38:21,836 --> 00:38:23,969
You cook it,
it's what I'm paying for.

595
00:38:25,371 --> 00:38:27,373
This car, happily,
has a more

596
00:38:27,408 --> 00:38:29,206
back-to-basics approach.

597
00:38:29,207 --> 00:38:32,043
In the M2, you get a little
rocker switch down here

598
00:38:32,078 --> 00:38:34,711
which does nothing at all

599
00:38:34,746 --> 00:38:37,814
as far as I can see,
and that's it.

600
00:38:37,849 --> 00:38:40,786
You're buying a car
which was set up at the factory

601
00:38:40,821 --> 00:38:44,490
by engineers who know
what they're doing.

602
00:38:44,525 --> 00:38:47,025
And all you have to do
is enjoy it.

603
00:38:49,227 --> 00:38:51,227
Which I am doing.

604
00:38:57,002 --> 00:38:58,988
Yes, look at this.

605
00:38:58,989 --> 00:39:00,940
Oh, yeah, loving that.

606
00:39:00,941 --> 00:39:02,740
[CHUCKLES]

607
00:39:02,741 --> 00:39:05,611
I'll do that again, actually,
and make sure-

608
00:39:05,646 --> 00:39:07,879
make sure I was correct.

609
00:39:15,421 --> 00:39:17,789
There's hardly
any understeer at all.

610
00:39:17,824 --> 00:39:20,158
Poke the back out
and hold it there.

611
00:39:20,193 --> 00:39:22,959
Yeah!
[LAUGHS]

612
00:39:22,994 --> 00:39:24,847
I'll do that again...

613
00:39:24,848 --> 00:39:26,631
only going the other way.

614
00:39:26,632 --> 00:39:28,767
Wrong way round
Your Name Here.

615
00:39:31,570 --> 00:39:36,139
Klaus and Fritz,
I am loving your work.

616
00:39:36,174 --> 00:39:39,945
Oh, I made a mess of that.
But I'll just do this,

617
00:39:39,980 --> 00:39:42,614
and it'll look like
I did it on purpose.

618
00:39:44,082 --> 00:39:46,518
With the tires begging
for mercy,

619
00:39:46,553 --> 00:39:48,218
I got back onto the Isn't

620
00:39:48,219 --> 00:39:52,055
and unleashed
365 rampaging horsepowers

621
00:39:52,090 --> 00:39:55,761
from the turbo-charged
three-liter straight six.

622
00:39:57,496 --> 00:40:00,466
This isn't as fast
in a straight line

623
00:40:00,501 --> 00:40:03,234
as an M3,
but everywhere else...

624
00:40:03,269 --> 00:40:05,069
it is better.

625
00:40:05,070 --> 00:40:08,540
It feels less heavy,
less cumbersome.

626
00:40:14,079 --> 00:40:17,616
And due to the unique
characteristics of our straight,

627
00:40:17,651 --> 00:40:20,552
I can tell the steering
is less...

628
00:40:20,587 --> 00:40:22,921
It's less twitchy.

629
00:40:27,593 --> 00:40:31,296
This track was designed
to trip cars up.

630
00:40:31,331 --> 00:40:33,465
There are fast corners,

631
00:40:33,500 --> 00:40:35,332
slow corners,

632
00:40:35,333 --> 00:40:37,752
drifty corners, and bumps.

633
00:40:37,787 --> 00:40:40,136
It's hard on the tires.

634
00:40:40,171 --> 00:40:43,775
It's hard on the brakes.
It's hard on the engine.

635
00:40:43,810 --> 00:40:47,546
It's point and squirt
and bark and yelp.

636
00:40:47,581 --> 00:40:49,681
It is vicious.

637
00:40:52,117 --> 00:40:56,855
But there is nothing here
which has flummoxed the M2.

638
00:40:56,890 --> 00:41:00,559
This thing is
an absolute masterpiece.

639
00:41:03,394 --> 00:41:06,532
Like I said,
it is the best M car

640
00:41:06,567 --> 00:41:08,567
BMW has ever made.

641
00:41:11,236 --> 00:41:13,721
You may think
it's mad to suggest

642
00:41:13,756 --> 00:41:16,207
the cheapest M car
is the best,

643
00:41:16,242 --> 00:41:18,042
but look at it this way,

644
00:41:18,043 --> 00:41:21,112
this track is a lot cheaper
than Silverstone.

645
00:41:21,147 --> 00:41:24,182
And I know which one
I'd rather drive round.

646
00:41:30,388 --> 00:41:32,558
[CHEERING]

647
00:41:36,427 --> 00:41:38,463
Go drive an M2.
Unbelievable.

648
00:41:38,498 --> 00:41:41,532
What?

649
00:41:41,567 --> 00:41:45,068
Why is there that zigzag
at Old Lady's House?

650
00:41:45,103 --> 00:41:47,272
- That wasn't there when I went.
- No, that wasn't.

651
00:41:47,307 --> 00:41:49,708
But after you'd left,
we discovered there is

652
00:41:49,743 --> 00:41:52,075
an unexploded Second
World War bomb, seriously,

653
00:41:52,110 --> 00:41:55,914
right where the track was,
so we had to go round it.

654
00:41:55,949 --> 00:41:58,584
- Fair enough.
- Let me get this right.

655
00:41:58,619 --> 00:42:01,219
At our track,
there's trees, animals,

656
00:42:01,254 --> 00:42:03,053
moisture, electricity,

657
00:42:03,054 --> 00:42:05,055
a house,
and an unexploded bomb?

658
00:42:05,090 --> 00:42:08,026
Yeah. Like I said, it's the most
dangerous track in the world.

659
00:42:08,061 --> 00:42:11,931
Honestly, it makes Imola
look like a duvet. Honestly.

660
00:42:11,966 --> 00:42:15,801
Anyway, all the cars that
we test at the Eboladrome

661
00:42:15,836 --> 00:42:17,635
will do a timed lap.

662
00:42:17,636 --> 00:42:20,205
And to make sure it's a
completely level playing field,

663
00:42:20,240 --> 00:42:24,175
they will all be driven
by the same racing driver.

664
00:42:24,210 --> 00:42:26,010
AUDIENCE: Ooh!

665
00:42:26,011 --> 00:42:28,246
We thought about this.
We gave it some thought.

666
00:42:28,281 --> 00:42:30,182
How clever is that? Now...

667
00:42:30,183 --> 00:42:31,616
[LAUGHTER]

668
00:42:31,617 --> 00:42:33,950
The thing is,
Amazon insisted

669
00:42:33,985 --> 00:42:35,854
that the racing driver
in question

670
00:42:35,855 --> 00:42:38,791
should be from this side
of the pond.

671
00:42:38,826 --> 00:42:41,692
So we went to something
called NASCAR?

672
00:42:41,727 --> 00:42:44,327
And we found one.
We shipped him to England

673
00:42:44,362 --> 00:42:47,633
and then we introduced him
to the complicated procedure

674
00:42:47,668 --> 00:42:50,286
which involves turning right.

675
00:42:50,321 --> 00:42:52,869
He's called Mike Skinner,

676
00:42:52,904 --> 00:42:55,506
but we know him simply as
"the American."

677
00:42:55,541 --> 00:42:57,207
We should warn you

678
00:42:57,208 --> 00:43:00,143
that some of his views
are quite strong.

679
00:43:00,178 --> 00:43:02,846
Yes, he has very specific
views, for example,

680
00:43:02,881 --> 00:43:05,283
on cars that have engines
with fewer than eight cylinders.

681
00:43:05,318 --> 00:43:07,450
- Communist.
- Or more than eight cylinders.

682
00:43:07,485 --> 00:43:10,722
- Communist.
- Or cars that have their engine in the middle.

683
00:43:10,757 --> 00:43:12,723
Communist.

684
00:43:12,724 --> 00:43:15,827
Everything that isn't American
really with a V8 is communist.

685
00:43:15,862 --> 00:43:18,897
So let's see how he gets on
with the M2.

686
00:43:18,932 --> 00:43:20,897
There he is.

687
00:43:20,932 --> 00:43:23,268
Looking utterly bewildered.

688
00:43:25,003 --> 00:43:28,271
But he's off.
A skitter of a wheel spin.

689
00:43:28,306 --> 00:43:30,707
And immediately
he's onto the Isn't Straight.

690
00:43:30,742 --> 00:43:33,211
There is a right there
that will have confused him.

691
00:43:33,246 --> 00:43:34,780
Now a left,
he'll be good at that.

692
00:43:34,781 --> 00:43:37,516
Now he can open the taps.

693
00:43:37,551 --> 00:43:40,216
Three liters, six cylinders.

694
00:43:40,251 --> 00:43:41,987
This thing wouldn't pull
a greasy string

695
00:43:41,988 --> 00:43:43,854
out of a dog's ass.

696
00:43:43,855 --> 00:43:48,727
JEREMY: I've literally no
idea what he's talking about.

697
00:43:48,762 --> 00:43:51,462
Where I come from,
this ain't a car.

698
00:43:51,497 --> 00:43:53,515
It's a cry for help.

699
00:43:53,550 --> 00:43:55,532
JEREMY: Whatever.

700
00:43:55,533 --> 00:43:57,903
He's through the final corner
on the Isn't Straight.

701
00:43:57,938 --> 00:44:00,706
Into Your Name Here.
Hard on the brakes.

702
00:44:00,741 --> 00:44:02,707
Grabbing the stick,

703
00:44:02,708 --> 00:44:04,710
which is on the left-hand
side of where he's sitting,

704
00:44:04,745 --> 00:44:06,176
and turning in.

705
00:44:06,177 --> 00:44:08,329
See what I mean?
No understeer at all.

706
00:44:08,364 --> 00:44:10,446
Damn tracks are narrower
than my driveway.

707
00:44:10,481 --> 00:44:13,284
Yeah, that's 'cause it doesn't
have a monster truck on it.

708
00:44:13,319 --> 00:44:17,120
Wonder which one of them
came up with this thing.

709
00:44:17,155 --> 00:44:20,358
The tall one? The short one?
Or the one with the girly hair?

710
00:44:20,393 --> 00:44:23,561
JEREMY: Okay, coming up
to Old Lady's House.

711
00:44:23,596 --> 00:44:25,029
Hard on the brakes.

712
00:44:25,030 --> 00:44:27,933
Turning in, round
the unexploded bomb,

713
00:44:27,968 --> 00:44:30,568
and now opening
the taps again.

714
00:44:30,603 --> 00:44:33,069
No turbo lag at all,

715
00:44:33,104 --> 00:44:35,506
as he hammers down
the bumpy back straight

716
00:44:35,541 --> 00:44:37,393
towards Sub Station.

717
00:44:37,394 --> 00:44:39,176
This is a 90-left,

718
00:44:39,177 --> 00:44:41,045
new Tarmac
but it held it well.

719
00:44:41,046 --> 00:44:44,115
One more 90-left to go
through Field Of Sheep.

720
00:44:44,150 --> 00:44:47,185
And he's done it well.
And across the line.

721
00:44:47,220 --> 00:44:49,220
[CHEERING]

722
00:44:50,956 --> 00:44:53,624
First one. That's good.

723
00:44:53,659 --> 00:44:55,192
He's great.

724
00:44:55,193 --> 00:44:58,163
He's great going
left and right. Amazing.

725
00:44:58,198 --> 00:45:00,030
To make sure that the M2-

726
00:45:00,031 --> 00:45:02,933
that wasn't on the lap board
all by itself,

727
00:45:02,968 --> 00:45:05,969
we got the American to put
other benchmark cars-

728
00:45:06,004 --> 00:45:10,141
we call them benchmark cars,
he calls them communist-

729
00:45:10,176 --> 00:45:12,176
around the track,
to see what's what.

730
00:45:12,177 --> 00:45:14,146
There they all are:
McLaren, Audi and so forth.

731
00:45:14,147 --> 00:45:15,999
Hang on a minute.
We saw a Ferrari 488

732
00:45:16,000 --> 00:45:17,817
at the track
in the film earlier on.

733
00:45:17,818 --> 00:45:19,016
Why isn't that on there?

734
00:45:19,017 --> 00:45:21,186
Because Ferrari
wouldn't let us time it.

735
00:45:21,221 --> 00:45:23,087
- Wouldn't let us?
- No.

736
00:45:23,088 --> 00:45:25,791
So the Ferrari LaFerrari
can't go on the road

737
00:45:25,826 --> 00:45:27,977
- or run on electric only?
- No.

738
00:45:28,012 --> 00:45:30,093
And the 488
can't do a timed lap?

739
00:45:30,128 --> 00:45:33,014
Ferrari are quite tricky to
deal with from time to time.

740
00:45:33,049 --> 00:45:35,901
Let's just put it that way
and move on. It's time to see

741
00:45:35,936 --> 00:45:38,504
where the M2 goes
on that board.

742
00:45:38,539 --> 00:45:41,072
Let's have a look.
Let's put it on.

743
00:45:41,107 --> 00:45:43,308
- Oh!
- RICHARD: Ha-ha!

744
00:45:43,343 --> 00:45:44,975
Wow! Wow!

745
00:45:44,976 --> 00:45:48,814
"I think it's the best M car
they've ever made."

746
00:45:50,448 --> 00:45:52,482
Not my words.

747
00:45:52,517 --> 00:45:54,853
"I prefer it to all the others
that are quicker than it."

748
00:45:54,888 --> 00:45:57,788
- Yes, it's-
- The one you should have,

749
